# Cloudflare Workers static-assets redirects (wrangler.jsonc assets dir = ./;
# host of record since 2026-07-17). Every rule here targets the CLEAN
# extensionless path — the site's one canonical URL shape. Locked end to end by
# tests/test_url_shape.py.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECTION 1 — retired lane page (2026-07-17 two-path restructure)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Home Repair was folded into the residential page as an in-page section. nginx
# (the Railway fallback) carries the same redirect WITH the #home-repair
# fragment; fragment support is undocumented for CF-style _redirects files, so
# it is intentionally omitted here.
#
# Both targets are the CLEAN path. Targeting `/residential-construction.html`
# made the old URL chain 301 -> 307 -> 200 (verified live 2026-08-03) — a
# wasted hop that dilutes the redirect signal and slows every legacy inbound
# link.
/home-repair.html /residential-construction 301
/home-repair /residential-construction 301

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECTION 2 — `.html` -> clean path, PERMANENT (added 2026-08-12)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cloudflare's `html_handling` already sends the `.html` form to the clean
# path, but it does so with a **307 Temporary Redirect** (reproduced locally on
# wrangler dev 4.122.0, and matching the live behaviour recorded 2026-08-03).
#
# A 307 is the wrong signal for a permanent URL migration:
#   - Google treats it as temporary, so the `.html` URL stays a live candidate
#     in the index instead of consolidating into the clean path.
#   - Ranking signals from legacy inbound links are not fully passed through a
#     temporary redirect the way they are through a 301.
# The sitemap was submitted to a Search Console Domain property on 2026-07-19
# and the first crawl is still settling, so making this permanent BEFORE
# indexing hardens is the whole point.
#
# An explicit `_redirects` rule takes precedence over `html_handling`
# (verified: the same path returns 307 without these lines and 301 with them),
# and query strings are preserved through the hop — which the money-path
# `?intent=` / `?utm_*` prefill in big7.js depends on.
#
# Every rule is exactly ONE hop: the target is an extensionless path that
# serves 200 directly and is never itself a redirect source. Adding a rule
# whose target appears as another rule's source would build a chain — do not.
/index.html / 301
/commercial-industrial.html /commercial-industrial 301
/residential-construction.html /residential-construction 301
/south-fulton-distribution.html /south-fulton-distribution 301
/accessibility.html /accessibility 301
